  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure builds in behind the a cold front for today, \nkeeping things mostly dry after early morning valley fog lifts.\nA spotty light shower or two can't be ruled out along the \nimmediate coast or southern NH today and tonight due to closer \nproximity to a weak low/surface trough developing south of Long \nIsland but the vast majority of the time should be dry. The most\nsunshine will be seen in the interior. Dewpoint values should be\nlower than what we felt on Thursday making for a more comfortable\nday with highs around 80 most locations. Valley fog is once again\nexpected tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn initially stalled front well south of New England will start its \nclimb north towards the region as a warm front on Saturday, bringing \nincreasing humidity. One complexity to this setup is two \nseparate waves of moisture; one originating from the Southeast \nU.S. on low-level southerly flow, while from the west will be a \n500 mb trough amplifying, and likely resulting in a surface low \ndeveloping. Guidance is beginning to hone in on the outcome of \nthe first wave clipping our coastal regions on Saturday, with \nthe upper- level low bringing more widespread rain chances, but \npotentially focused in the interior, on Sunday. It will be a \nfavorable environment for efficient rainfall with PWATs above \n1.5\", dew points in the upper 60s, and some measurable IVT \ntransport. However, scattered coverage to the precipitation with\nmoderate storm motions should preclude a significant threat for\nexcessive rainfall, and it is likely some locations see limited\nrainfall given the hit-or-miss nature.\n\nClouds and precipitation should allow highs to trend a bit lower \nthan the last few days for the weekend (widespread 70s); however \nwith increased humidity and mild nighttime lows, it will not feel \nnotably \"cooler\" outside.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe upper-level low to our west takes over as the main driver of \nlocal weather to start next week, with current indications that it \nmay fully close off and stall over Quebec. This brings cyclonic \nwesterly flow to New England to start the week. A dry punch in the \nmid-levels combined with downsloping westerly flow should bring \npleasant conditions to the coastal plain with sunshine and \nseasonable temperatures in the mid-upper 70s, while dew points fall \nto the 40s/50s. With the upper-level low still lingering to our \nnorth however, there will likely be some upslope-driven clouds and \nvertically-shallow precipitation in the White Mountains and \nsurrounding higher-terrain.",
